The concatenation of the extrinsic and intrinsic transformation matrices yields the desired transformation matrix, which transforms world coordinates to rendered pixel coordinates:

$$\lambda
    \begin{bmatrix}
    x \\
    y \\
    1
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    x' \\
    y' \\
    w
    \end{bmatrix}
=
\mathbf{K} \mathbf{M}_2 \mathbf{M}_1 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    X \\
    Y \\
    Z \\
    1
    \end{bmatrix}
$$

Converting back to heterogeneous from homogeneous coordinates, the 2D pixel indices of a 3D point $[X, Y, Z]^\mathsf{T}$ are $[x,y]^\mathsf{T}=[x'/w, y'/w]^\mathsf{T}$. We can make all the matrices in the equation explicit and write:

$$\begin{bmatrix}    x' \\
    y' \\
    w
    \end{bmatrix}
=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    a & 0 & c_x & 0 \\
    0 & a & c_y & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1 & 0
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    R_1 & R_2 & R_3  & 0 \\
    R_4 & R_5 & R_6 & 0 \\
    R_7 & R_8 & R_9 & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & 0 & -T_X \\
    0 & 1 & 0 & -T_Y \\
    0 & 0 & 1 & -T_Z \\
    0 & 0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    X \\
    Y \\
    Z \\
    1
    \end{bmatrix}
    $$

The right-most matrix multiplications reflect the camera's extrinsic parameters, and the left-most matrix reflects the intrinsic parameters and the perspective projection. Note that the column of zeros in the left-most matrix means that, without loss of generality, the equation can be written in a slightly simpler form as:

$$\begin{bmatrix}    x' \\
    y' \\
    w
    \end{bmatrix}
=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    a & 0 & c_x  \\
    0 & a & c_y  \\
    0 & 0 & 1 
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    R_1 & R_2 & R_3   \\
    R_4 & R_5 & R_6  \\
    R_7 & R_8 & R_9 
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & 0 & -T_X \\
    0 & 1 & 0 & -T_Y \\
    0 & 0 & 1 & -T_Z
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    X \\
    Y \\
    Z \\
    1
    \end{bmatrix}
    $$

